    <!-- 血糖等级专家建议文案数组 -->
    <string-array name="bs_level_expert_advice">
        <!-- 低血糖建议 -->
        <item>Your blood sugar level is &lt;font color="#3489FE"&gt;&lt;b&gt;low&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;, also known as hypoglycemia, it\'s important to take immediate action to raise your blood sugar. Here are some simple suggestions: &lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Consume a fast-acting source of glucose:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt; Consume 15 to 20 grams of fast-acting carbohydrates to raise your blood sugar quickly. Examples include glucose tablets or gel, fruit juice, regular soda, or a small serving of raisins. &lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Check your blood sugar after 15 minutes:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt; If it is still low, repeat the process. &lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Follow up with a balanced snack or meal:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt; Once your blood sugar has risen, it\'s important to stabilize it with a balanced snack or meal containing protein, complex carbohydrates, and healthy fats. This will help maintain your blood sugar at a normal level and prevent another drop. Good options include whole-grain crackers with peanut butter, a small sandwich, or a piece of fruit with a handful of nuts. &lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Discuss with your healthcare provider:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt; If you frequently experience low blood sugar, it\'s important to discuss it with your healthcare provider. They can evaluate the underlying cause and adjust your treatment plan accordingly. They may recommend changes in your medication regimen, diet, or exercise routine to better manage your blood sugar levels.</item>
        
        <!-- 正常血糖建议 -->
        <item>To help manage your blood sugar levels and keep them within a &lt;font color="#02BC77"&gt;&lt;b&gt;normal&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt; range, here are some simple suggestions: &lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Follow a balanced and healthy diet:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt; Opt for a balanced diet that includes a variety of fruits, vegetables, whole grains, lean proteins, and healthy fats. Limit your intake of sugary and processed foods. Focus on portion control and consider working with a registered dietitian to develop a personalized meal plan that suits your specific needs. &lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Engage in regular physical activity:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt; Regular exercise can help improve insulin sensitivity and regulate blood sugar levels. Aim for at least 150 minutes of moderate-intensity aerobic activity, such as brisk walking or cycling, spread throughout the week. Additionally, incorporate strength training exercises to build muscle mass and further support blood sugar management. &lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Monitor your blood sugar levels:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt; Regularly check your blood sugar levels as advised by your healthcare provider. This will help you understand how your body responds to different foods, activities, and medications. By monitoring consistently, you can make necessary adjustments to your diet, exercise, or medication regimen to keep your blood sugar within the target range.</item>
        
        <!-- 糖尿病前期建议 -->
        <item>Managing &lt;font color="#FFB901"&gt;&lt;b&gt;prediabetes&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt; is essential to prevent or delay the onset of diabetes. Here are some simple suggestions: &lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Make dietary changes:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt; Adopt a balanced and nutritious diet to help control your blood sugar levels. Focus on consuming whole foods, including fruits, vegetables, whole grains, lean proteins, and healthy fats. Limit your intake of processed foods, sugary drinks, and refined carbohydrates. Consider working with a registered dietitian to create a personalized meal plan that suits your specific needs. &lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Increase fiber intake:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt; Include high-fiber foods in your diet, such as whole grains, legumes, vegetables, and fruits. Fiber helps regulate blood sugar levels, improves satiety, and supports overall digestive health. &lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Manage stress:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt; Chronic stress can impact blood sugar levels and overall health. Find healthy ways to manage stress, such as practicing relaxation techniques, engaging in hobbies, exercising, or seeking support from friends, family, or a mental health professional.</item>
        
        <!-- 糖尿病建议 -->
        <item>When you have &lt;font color="#FA0202"&gt;&lt;b&gt;diabetes&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;, it\'s crucial to manage your condition effectively to maintain stable blood sugar levels and prevent complications. &lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Maintain regular healthcare appointments:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt; Regularly visit your healthcare provider to monitor your diabetes and receive necessary medical care. These appointments may include routine check-ups, blood tests, eye exams, and foot exams. Your healthcare provider will also review your medications, offer education, and provide guidance for managing your condition effectively. &lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Keep a diabetes management journal:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt; Maintain a journal to track your daily activities, meals, blood sugar readings, medication doses, and any notable symptoms or patterns. This can help you identify trends, understand how different factors affect your blood sugar levels, and make informed adjustments to your diabetes management plan. &lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Plan ahead for sick days:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt; Illness or infections can impact blood sugar levels and require adjustments to your diabetes management plan. Develop a sick-day plan in collaboration with your healthcare provider, which includes guidelines for monitoring blood sugar, adjusting medication dosages if necessary, staying hydrated, and seeking medical attention if symptoms worsen.</item>
    </string-array>


    <string-array name="bp_level_expert_advice">
        <!-- Low Blood Pressure -->
        <item>
            <![CDATA[
            Your blood pressure reading of <font color="#3487FC"><b>%1$d/%2$d</b></font> is <font color="#3487FC"><b>low</b></font>, here are some suggestions to consider:

            <br><b>Stay hydrated:</b><br>
            Drink an adequate amount of water throughout the day to help maintain blood volume and prevent dehydration, which can contribute to low blood pressure.

            <br><b>Increase salt intake:</b><br>
            Consume a slightly higher amount of salt, but within recommended limits, as salt can help retain fluid and raise blood pressure. Consult with a healthcare professional to determine the appropriate salt intake for your situation.

            <br><b>Wear compression stockings:</b><br>
            Compression stockings can help improve circulation and prevent blood from pooling in your legs, thereby raising blood pressure.

            <br><b>Eat small, frequent meals:</b><br>
            Instead of large meals, opt for smaller, more frequent meals throughout the day. This can help prevent a drop in blood pressure after eating.

            <br><b>Avoid sudden position changes:</b><br>
            Avoid getting up too quickly from a seated or lying position, as this can cause a sudden drop in blood pressure. Take your time and allow your body to adjust gradually.
            ]]>
        </item>

        <!-- Normal Blood Pressure -->
        <item>
            <![CDATA[
            Your blood pressure reading of <font color="#05BA7B"><b>%1$d/%2$d</b></font> is within the <font color="#05BA7B"><b>normal range</b></font>, which is great for your heart and overall health. Here are some suggestions to maintain it:

            <br><b>Eat healthy:</b><br>
            Maintain a balanced diet rich in fruits, vegetables, lean proteins, and whole grains.

            <br><b>Stay active:</b><br>
            Exercise regularly (at least 150 minutes of moderate activity per week).

            <br><b>Control sodium:</b><br>
            Limit sodium and processed food intake to prevent future increases.

            <br><b>Manage stress:</b><br>
            Practice relaxation techniques such as meditation, yoga, or deep breathing.

            <br><b>Routine check-ups:</b><br>
            Schedule regular health check-ups to keep track of your blood pressure.
            ]]>
        </item>

        <!-- Elevated Blood Pressure -->
        <item>
            <![CDATA[
            Your blood pressure reading of <font color="#FFE902"><b>%1$d/%2$d</b></font> is <font color="#FFE902"><b>elevated</b></font>, which means you are at risk of developing hypertension if no lifestyle changes are made. Here are some suggestions to consider:

            <br><b>Reduce salt:</b><br>
            Cut back on salty and high-sodium foods.

            <br><b>Increase activity:</b><br>
            Add more physical exercise, such as walking, cycling, or swimming.

            <br><b>Maintain weight:</b><br>
            Keep a healthy body weight, as even small changes help.

            <br><b>Limit stimulants:</b><br>
            Reduce alcohol and caffeine intake to avoid spikes.

            <br><b>Monitor regularly:</b><br>
            Check your blood pressure often to catch changes early.
            ]]>
        </item>

        <!-- Stage 1 Hypertension -->
        <item>
            <![CDATA[
            Your blood pressure reading of <font color="#FFB909"><b>%1$d/%2$d</b></font> falls into <font color="#FFB909"><b>Stage 1 Hypertension</b></font>. Lifestyle changes are crucial to prevent progression. Here are some suggestions to consider:

            <br><b>Adopt the DASH diet:</b><br>
            Focus on fresh, low-sodium foods and avoid processed meals.

            <br><b>Stay active daily:</b><br>
            Engage in regular exercise and reduce sitting time.

            <br><b>Quit smoking:</b><br>
            Avoid tobacco as it raises blood pressure and damages vessels.

            <br><b>Improve sleep:</b><br>
            Aim for 7–9 hours of quality rest each night.

            <br><b>Consult a doctor:</b><br>
            Seek medical advice to determine if medication is needed.
            ]]>
        </item>

        <!-- Stage 2 Hypertension -->
        <item>
            <![CDATA[
            Your blood pressure reading of <font color="#FF8000"><b>%1$d/%2$d</b></font> is in <font color="#FF8000"><b>Stage 2 Hypertension</b></font>, which significantly increases your risk of heart attack and stroke. Here are some suggestions to consider:

            <br><b>Follow treatment:</b><br>
            Work with your doctor, including possible medication.

            <br><b>Strict diet control:</b><br>
            Limit salt, fried foods, and sugary drinks.

            <br><b>Exercise safely:</b><br>
            Stay active but avoid intense workouts without clearance.

            <br><b>Stress management:</b><br>
            Use relaxation techniques and ensure good sleep.

            <br><b>Home monitoring:</b><br>
            Measure blood pressure daily and report spikes to your doctor.
            ]]>
        </item>

        <!-- Hypertensive Crisis -->
        <item>
            <![CDATA[
            Your blood pressure reading of <font color="#FB0301"><b>%1$d/%2$d</b></font> indicates a <font color="#FB0301"><b>Hypertension Crisis</b></font>, which is a medical emergency. Here are some immediate actions to consider:

            <br><b>Seek emergency help:</b><br>
            Call emergency services or go to the ER immediately.

            <br><b>Take medications:</b><br>
            Use prescribed medication exactly as directed.

            <br><b>Stay calm:</b><br>
            Sit quietly and avoid physical activity while waiting for help.

            <br><b>Avoid self-treatment:</b><br>
            Do not use over-the-counter remedies.

            <br><b>Keep records:</b><br>
            Bring recent readings and medication info for your healthcare team.
            ]]>
        </item>
    </string-array>

